oader audiences worldwide, striving to alleviate waste and poverty challenges.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/a-mexican-entrepreneur-began-melting-discarded-plastic-into-wall-and-roof-panels-within-three-years-his-startup-had-built-500-rooms-and-secured-contracts-for-300-homes/articleshow/133597987.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/a-mexican-entrepreneur-began-melting-discarded-plastic-into-wall-and-roof-panels-within-three-years-his-startup-had-built-500-rooms-and-secured-contracts-for-300-homes/articleshow/133597987.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-30T01:54:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133599529,imgsize-2512962.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Unfinished for nearly 30 years, Hollywood Video’s founder's Oregon mansion is up for $4.7 million]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133566223.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[A nearly 52,000-square-foot mansion in West Linn, Oregon, has remained unfinished for almost 30 years and is now listed for $4.7 million. Hollywood Video founder Mark Wattles bought the 32-acre property for $1.25 million in 1994 and spent about $12 million on the project. Two later owners also had plans for the estate, but the mansion was never completed.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1994-hollywood-videos-founder-bought-32-oregon-acres-for-1-25-million-and-began-a-52000-sq-ft-mansion-nearly-30-years-and-3-owners-later-it-is-still-unfinished-and-asking-4-7-million/articleshow/133566223.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1994-hollywood-videos-founder-bought-32-oregon-acres-for-1-25-million-and-began-a-52000-sq-ft-mansion-nearly-30-years-and-3-owners-later-it-is-still-unfinished-and-asking-4-7-million/articleshow/133566223.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-30T01:49:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133566826,imgsize-174602.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[In 2020, four engineers started Moment Energy in a British Columbia garage; 6 years later...]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133599552.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Moment Energy has launched its most extensive second-life battery manufacturing plant, transforming used electric vehicle batteries into essential energy storage systems. This innovative facility aims to deliver backup power for various crucial facilities across North America. The company has successfully obtained considerable funding and safety certifications, while also planning to broaden its manufacturing operations into Texas.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-2020-four-engineers-started-moment-energy-in-a-british-columbia-garage-6-years-later-their-new-plant-is-repurposing-retired-ev-batteries-for-data-centres-and-hospitals/articleshow/133599552.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/in-2020-four-engineers-started-moment-energy-in-a-british-columbia-garage-6-years-later-their-new-plant-is-repurposing-retired-ev-batteries-for-data-centres-and-hospitals/articleshow/133599552.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-30T00:55:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133599606,imgsize-1891517.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Private landowner protects 145 acres beside Zion from future development]]></tit